    "q-37855": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37855",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
